>&#x003c3;</italic><sub><italic>&#x003b2;</italic></sub>), probe-test variability (<italic>&#x003c3;</italic><sub><italic>&#x003b3;</italic></sub>) and within-probe variability (<italic>&#x003c3;</italic><sub><italic>&#x003b5;</italic></sub>). The column <inline-formula><mml:math xmlns:mml="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" id="M2" name="1471-2105-9-261-i1" overflow="scroll"><mml:semantics><mml:mrow><mml:msub><mml:mover accent="true"><mml:mi>x</mml:mi><mml:mo>&#x000af;</mml:mo></mml:mover><mml:mrow><mml:mi>a</mml:mi><mml:mi>l</mml:mi><mml:mi>t</mml:mi></mml:mrow></mml:msub></mml:mrow></mml:semantics></mml:math></inline-formula> indicates the mean number of simulated altered probes.</p></table-wrap-foot></table-wrap><table-wrap position="float" id="T2"><label>Table 2</label><caption><p>Empirical type-I error and power obtained in 1,000 simulations using the three different approaches: REX (iterative regression), PEMM (probe-specific mixture model) and threshold.</p></caption><table frame="hsides" rules="groups"><thead><tr><td></td><td></td><td></td><td></td><td align="center" colspan="2">Type-I error</td><td align="center" colspan="3">Power (gains)</td><td align="center" colspan="3">Power (loses)</td></tr><tr><td colspan="4"></td><td colspan="2"><hr></hr></td><td colspan="3"><hr></hr></td><td colspan="3"><hr></hr></td></tr><tr><td align="center"><italic>&#x003c3;</italic><sub><italic>&#x003b2;</italic></sub></td><td align="center"><italic>&#x003c3;</italic><sub><italic>&#x003b3;</italic></sub></td><td align="center"><italic>&#x003c3;</italic><sub><italic>&#x003b5;</italic></sub></td><td align="center"><inline-formula><mml:math xmlns:mml="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" id="M3" name="1471-2105-9-261-i1" overflow="scroll"><mml:semantics><mml:mrow><mml:msub><mml:mover accent="true"><mml:mi>x</mml:mi><mml:mo>&#x000af;</mml:mo></mml:mover><mml:mrow><mml:mi>a</mml:mi><mml:mi>l</mml:mi><mml:mi>t</mml:mi></mml:mrow></mml:msub></mml:mrow></mml:semantics></mml:math></inl